[Music]  welcome back to science clique today  supercritical fluids  mata can exist in different states  in our daily lives on earth there are  three states of matter  solid liquid and gas  each of these three forms corresponds to  a microscopic configuration  in a solid molecules are fixed in  relation to each other they have a  regular structure because they are  strongly bound  in a liquid molecules are more loosely  bound they are free to move around but  they maintain a certain cohesion  a liquid can be deformed but it  conserves a certain volume  this contrasts with gases in which  molecules are free to move around and  eventually take up the entire volume in  which they are contained  depending on the temperature and  pressure of the environment a body will  naturally be in one of these three  states  for instance at atmospheric pressure and  in the range between 0 and 100 degrees  celsius water is naturally liquid  these are the conditions of our  atmosphere and explains why liquid water  can be found on the planet  if the temperature were 200 degrees  celsius keeping pressure constant water  would be found as a gas  water vapor  in order to model the conditions  required for the presence of each of the  three states of mata we can draw a phase  diagram  such a diagram represents the phase of  water in other words the state in which  it occurs naturally for different values  of pressure and temperature  [Music]  we start by taking a tightly sealed  container into which we will pour water  the temperature inside the container can  be adjusted through heating or cooling  and the pressure can be altered by  pressing or pulling a piston  to fill in the diagram we will use three  colors we choose to use grey for gas  light blue for liquid  and dark blue for solid  each point of the diagram corresponds to  a unique temperature pressure  combination which we will replicate  inside the container  once the settings have been chosen we  can observe which phase the water inside  the container is in  and draw a small square on the diagram  with the corresponding color  by repeating this process for multiple  values we finally obtain a map on which  three zones appear  if a given pressure temperature pair  lands inside one of these zones for  example in the dark blue zone this means  that under these specific conditions  water is naturally in a solid state  one of the first things that stands out  is the fact that the three zones have  precise boundaries  when we are at the limit between two  zones the two states on either side of  the boundary can co-exist simultaneously  in a stable way  for instance when we heat up an ice cube  under atmospheric pressure it gradually  moves to the right of the diagram as its  temperature increases  when we reach the limit of the solid  zone a temperature of zero degrees  celsius the very first drops of liquid  water appear  gradually the ice cube melts while  remaining at the same temperature  the energy supplied through heating no  longer changes its temperature but  instead causes it to melt to rearrange  its molecules on a microscopic scale  once the ice cube is entirely melted its  temperature will start to rise again  signaling a movement into the zone of  the diagram corresponding to the liquid  state  we have performed a phase transition  going from one state of mata to another  by crossing one of these boundary curves  another point which deserves mentioning  is that there exists a point at which  the three zones touch  this is a point of contact between each  possible state of matter  solid liquid and gas  under these specific conditions of  temperature and pressure it is thus  possible for water to coexist in a solid  liquid and gas form all at once  this peculiar setting is called the  triple point  for water this triple point corresponds  to a pressure of 0.6 percent of an  atmosphere and a temperature of 100 of a  degree celsius  conditions that do not occur naturally  on earth but which we can create in a  lab allowing us to surprisingly boil  water while some of it is simultaneously  frozen  [Music]  coming back to the phase diagram what  interests us most is the fact that the  curve separating liquid and gas seems to  stop abruptly  this specific point where the curve  stops is the critical point  it corresponds to a very high  temperature and pressure combination  conditions that do not occur naturally  on earth but which can be reproduced in  a laboratory  let's take our water container and  adjust its pressure and temperature in  order to lie on this curve  as we have seen at the boundary between  the two zones water appears  simultaneously in two different states  liquid and gas  inside the container we see a separation  between liquid water and water vapor  let's now gradually heat and compress  the container so that we approach the  critical point while remaining on the  curve  as pressure increases the water  molecules in the gas are strongly  compressed  they come closer together and take up  less space  the vapor becomes more dense  on the other hand increasing the  temperature forces the molecules in the  liquid to vibrate more and more  in this way the molecules are less bound  to each other and their increasing  agitation makes them occupy more space  the liquid water becomes less dense  gradually the separation between the  liquid and the vapor becomes less and  less visible  suddenly the density of the liquid which  decreases and that of the gas which  increases reach the same value  from that moment on the two phases are  no longer distinct the liquid and the  gas in the container have exactly the  same density  we obtain a sort of homogeneous mixture  of liquid and gas we've reached the  critical point  beyond this point it is impossible to  distinguish the gas from the liquid  the two phases have merged into one  the state of this water is neither  liquid nor gas  its behavior is fairly similar to that  of a gas but it also exhibits  interesting properties that are usually  attributed to liquids  we call this state a supercritical fluid  it is interesting to note that unlike  the solid state liquid and gas are not  independent  although it is possible to go from  liquid to gas by transition by boiling  water  it is also possible to go from liquid to  gas without any such transition  to do this we simply adjust the  temperature and pressure in our  container in order to bypass the  boundary between the two states  we start with a liquid and by making it  a supercritical fluid we can transform  it into a gas  this experiment is astonishing to  observe  it is very unusual to witness a liquid  becoming a gas without any visible  transition  taking our supercritical fluid once  again we can observe a surprising  phenomenon as we approach the critical  point  as the fluid reaches this point it  spontaneously begins to scatter light  this is called critical opalescence  it is due to the refraction of light  within the fluid  light rays slow down depending on the  fluid's density  as it approaches the critical point the  fluid has to split into a liquid and a  gas  but just before this happens it  hesitates between one state and the  other  these hesitations within the fluid lead  to strong density fluctuations which  scatter light  in the same way that our atmosphere  scatters sunlight giving the sky its  colour  in our daily lives supercritical fluids  have many  applications their properties are very  interesting  for instance although they behave mainly  like a gas we can use them as solvents  they can serve to extract certain  substances  supercritical carbon dioxide  is widely used to extract flavors from  plants nicotine from tobacco or even  caffeine from coffee  a major advantage of supercritical co2  is that unlike liquid solvents it is  non-toxic and relatively easy to use  with a very high extraction power  in its supercritical form water is also  a very good solvent it is useful for  destroying organic waste without  releasing harmful gases as opposed to  burning it  supercritical fluids have a multitude of  diverse practical applications  such as fuel production  refrigeration  or even microbial protection  to conclude the supercritical fluid is a  state of matter in its own right  above a certain pressure and temperature  point  liquid and gas seem to merge  forming a single homogeneous state  with both gas and liquid-like properties  on earth temperature and pressure are  too low to allow for such fluids to  exist in a natural form  only small quantities can be found at  the bottom of the ocean within  hydrothermal vents  where temperature and pressure are  extreme  although rare on earth supercritical  fluids are relatively common in the  universe  especially inside gaseous planets  within their atmospheres the conditions  are so extreme that some layers are  actually supercritical  similarly the very hot and thick  atmosphere of venus can reach 500  degrees celsius and a pressure a hundred  times greater than on earth  under such conditions the atmosphere of  the planet consisting mainly of carbon  dioxide is a supercritical fluid  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

